C = [
("h1", "7. The Law Entered, and Grace Did Much More Abound"),
("p", "Claim: Romans 5:20–21 closes the passage with a sentence about the law and a sentence about two reigns. The first reports a purpose, and Paul answers at once the objection it raises. The second ends the passage on the name of the Lord. This chapter reads the two verses, the question that follows them in chapter 6, and what the law is said to do in the words Paul gives."),
("sub", "7.1 “The law entered”"),
("p", "“Moreover the law entered, that the offence might abound” (Romans 5:20). The verb is a small one. The law “entered.” It came in alongside what was already there. Verse 13 has said that sin was in the world before the law, and verse 14 that death reigned from Adam to Moses. The law is a later arrival, and it is said to have arrived for a purpose: “that the offence might abound.”"),
("p", "Paul has said the same sort of thing before. “By the law is the knowledge of sin” (Romans 3:20). “The law worketh wrath: for where no law is, there is no transgression” (Romans 4:15). The law gives knowledge, and it gives the thing that can be named a transgression. In chapter 7 Paul will say more: “I had not known sin, but by the law” (Romans 7:7), and “the commandment, which was ordained to life, I found to be unto death” (Romans 7:10). He is careful to say in the same place that the law is not the evil: “Wherefore the law is holy, and the commandment holy, and just, and good” (Romans 7:12)."),
("p", "The volume reports these statements and does not extend them. It does not say to whom the law was given, or what it covered, or where its authority stopped. Those are the questions of Study 59, The Law’s Jurisdiction and the Body of Christ, and that study should be read for them. The statement in Romans 5:20 is a narrower one. It says that the law entered, that it did so with a purpose, and that the purpose bore on the abounding of the offence."),
("sub", "7.2 “Grace did much more abound”"),
("p", "“But where sin abounded, grace did much more abound” (Romans 5:20). This is the third time the passage says “much more” of grace. It comes in verse 15, in verse 17, and here. The word is the strongest in the passage: the grace did not merely meet the sin at its measure. It exceeded it. Wherever sin abounded, grace abounded further."),
("p", "The saying has a danger, and Paul sees it. If the offence abounds under the law, and grace abounds all the more where sin abounds, a reader may conclude that sin is to be welcomed. Paul puts the objection in the next chapter as a question that he expects to be asked: “What shall we say then? Shall we continue in sin, that grace may abound?” (Romans 6:1). He answers: “God forbid. How shall we, that are dead to sin, live any longer therein?” (Romans 6:2)."),
("p", "The answer shows how Paul himself reads Romans 5:20. He does not say that the sentence means less than it says. He says that it does not justify the conclusion. The man to whom the grace has come is said to be “dead to sin.” The volume does not enter here into what chapter 6 goes on to teach. It takes from the question and its answer only the point that Paul reads the abounding of grace as a fact of the passage and not as a licence."),
("sub", "7.3 The two reigns (5:21)"),
("p", "“That as sin hath reigned unto death, even so might grace reign through righteousness unto eternal life by Jesus Christ our Lord” (Romans 5:21). The last verse is a purpose clause that gathers the passage. Sin has reigned, and its reign ended in death. Grace might reign, and its reign is through righteousness and ends in eternal life. The two reigns are set side by side as the two possible kingdoms of the passage, and the reader is shown which one ends in which result."),
("p", "The passage has spoken of reigning five times. The table lists them with the one who reigns, the means, and the end."),
("ctbl", "The reigns of Romans 5.", [
    ("Verse", "Who reigns, and how", "The end"),
    ("5:14", "Death reigned from Adam to Moses", "Over them that had not sinned after Adam’s transgression"),
    ("5:17", "Death reigned by one man’s offence", "The reign of death through one"),
    ("5:17", "They which receive shall reign in life by one, Jesus Christ", "The reign in life"),
    ("5:21", "Sin hath reigned unto death", "Death"),
    ("5:21", "Grace might reign through righteousness by Jesus Christ our Lord", "Eternal life"),
  ], [0.12, 0.58, 0.30]),
("p", "Three things are noticed. First, the subject changes. In verses 14 and 17 it is death that reigns. In verse 21 it is sin, and the final reign is that of grace. Death, sin and grace are the three powers of the passage, and two of them are set against the third. Second, the means of the reign of grace is “through righteousness.” The word that has been used throughout the passage as a gift is now the road along which grace reigns. Third, the end is “eternal life,” the only place the phrase appears in the passage. The passage began with death entering the world, and it ends with eternal life."),
("sub", "7.4 “Jesus Christ our Lord”"),
("p", "The passage ends on a personal note. The comparison had been about “one man” and “one,” and the second was “Jesus Christ” (Romans 5:15, 17). The last words of the passage give him his title and add a pronoun: “Jesus Christ our Lord.” The word “our” is in the text. The writer, who has been describing men as men, ends by speaking as one of a company who call this man Lord."),
("p", "The observation is small, and it has to be handled with care. It does not turn the whole passage into a statement about one company of persons rather than another. The passage has spoken of “all men” and “many.” It ends by saying whose Lord the second man is. The passage as a whole reads best as a statement about men as men, ending with the confession of those who have received the gift. The volume reports the ending as the ending."),
("sub", "7.5 The passage as a whole"),
("p", "With verse 21, the passage that began at verse 12 is complete. It has made, in order, these statements. Sin entered the world by one man, and death by sin, and death reached all men (verse 12). Sin was in the world before the law, and death reigned from Adam to Moses (verses 13 and 14). Adam is the figure of him that was to come, and the gift is not as the offence (verses 14 to 17). The comparison is resumed and completed: one offence, one righteousness; judgment, the free gift; condemnation, justification of life (verse 18). The many were made sinners and the many shall be made righteous (verse 19). The law entered, and grace did much more abound (verse 20). Sin reigned unto death, and grace might reign unto eternal life by Jesus Christ our Lord (verse 21)."),
("p", "The next five chapters turn to the other passage. Before they do, the reader should hold one thing from this one. The question the passage answers is the question of how sin, death and righteousness reached men. It does not ask what kind of body the dead will have. It does not mention the resurrection of the dead at all."),
]
